course: dictionary
lesson: 364
title: Anticipate: expecting it and getting ready
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To anticipate something is to expect it will happen and get ready for it. You can also anticipate someone's move, which means guessing what they will do and acting first. It can also mean looking forward to something nice.
explain: «Dogs learn patterns and can anticipate events, like a family member coming home.» The dog learns that a car or a key in the door means someone is coming, so it gets ready to greet them. The words around anticipate tell you the dog is expecting something, not just noticing it.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «The coach could anticipate where the ball would go.» If you swap in "could expect where the ball would go", it still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Always check with the words around the hard word.
fact: Anticipate means to expect something to happen and get ready for it.
fact: Anticipate can also mean to guess what someone will do and act first.
fact: «Eagerly anticipated» means waited for with excitement, looking forward to it.
fact: «As anticipated» means just as expected.
fact: Anticipate is a verb: anticipates, anticipated and anticipating are its other forms.
exercise: «Dogs can anticipate events, like a family member coming home.» What does anticipate mean here? | Forget about it | *Expect something and get ready | Whisper quietly | Chase it away
exercise: «The coach could anticipate where the ball would go.» What does anticipate mean? | Stop the ball with her hand | Ignore the ball completely | *Guess what will happen and act first | Kick the ball as hard as she can
exercise: Which word is the -ing form of anticipate? | anticipated | anticipates | *anticipating | anticipate
test: Sam says, «We eagerly anticipated the holidays.» What does eagerly anticipated mean? | Ignored completely | *Looked forward to with excitement | Dreaded with fear | Forgotten quickly
test: Which word means nearly the same as anticipate in «anticipate a problem»? | Cancel | Enjoy | Forget | *Expect
exam: Which best explains anticipate in «Dogs can anticipate events»? | It means the dog barks at strangers | *It means the dog expects something to happen and gets ready | It means the dog is hungry for dinner | It means the dog is asleep
